{"kind":"report","text":"DEPLOYMENT ROUND 11 CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head 347baa0332108ec99532649db9c95bac560e34b1, against request git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:49cf66149d235e0fe88db75c4182af96b49c9e1d and promise git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a05342efd0dbcadb5869b3c9259793a142ac56b9.\n\nThe generalized topology is sound: adding every admitted and refused candidate as a ledger parent does not change P, E, the claim, or any recovery row; it only adds reachability. A mixed-batch Git probe confirms previous-ledger + E + admitted candidate + refused candidate retains every object after inbox deletion, claim release, reflog expiry, and gc.\n\nThree blockers remain. First, case 4's stated prune sequence deletes the inbox refs but not the live claim. The claim itself already parents E and every candidate, so it masks a broken ledger. Probe /tmp/gitseq-deployment-r11.21SFbq built sequence, mirror, ledger, claim, admitted and refused candidates. With the refused parent deliberately missing from the ledger, the refused object was retained while the claim existed and pruned after claim deletion; with the repaired ledger parent it survived both (`missing-refused live-claim=retained after-release=pruned`; `repaired live-claim=retained after-release=retained`). The case must delete/release the claim before reflog expiry and GC, and enumerate refs to establish the ledger is the sole candidate retainer.\n\nSecond, lines 630-631 directly contradict the repair: after correctly requiring every refused candidate as a parent, the note says the all-refused ledger commit “still omits E and candidate parents.” It may omit redundant E=P, but it must not omit candidate parents.\n\nThird, the advertised bijection is not encoded by the shown line format. The prose says every line names a candidate parent by hash, but `identity=\u003cfour-part identity\u003e result=...` contains no candidate commit hash; the identity/digest is not the Git object ID. Add an explicit `candidate=\u003ccommit\u003e` field (and define duplicate/order handling) so the parent-to-line mapping is mechanically checkable.\n\nThe refusal replay claim also needs its actual immutable inputs. `size` depends on the genesis descriptor's ceiling, and `unknown-log` depends on the target/log context; neither is recomputable from the candidate object alone. Those inputs are durably reachable in this topology (P/genesis and the per-genesis ledger context), but the rule must say admission is rerun against the recorded P/genesis descriptor and the exact pinned admission profile/version, not merely “against [the candidate].” Without pinning the admission version, a later auditor can disagree after an admission-rule upgrade while both drain and auditor are correct for their eras. Shape/unsigned are candidate-local; size/unknown-log are not.\n\nFinally, the ledger branch's initial value/first-entry lease remains unspecified: every entry has a “previous ledger head,” but no initialization defines one. Specify a genesis ledger root or an absent-value first append so the first batch is executable and auditable. Diff check passes. Approval remains exact-head only.","body":{"head":"347baa0332108ec99532649db9c95bac560e34b1","probe":"/tmp/gitseq-deployment-r11.21SFbq","verdict":"changes-requested"}}